+ ### Wait for Completion (Default — Works with Any Agent)
+ The simplest and most reliable pattern. Blocks until CellCog finishes, then returns the full result including status, response, generated file paths, and credits — all in `result["message"]`.
+ # Blocks until done — returns full results
+ result = client.create_chat(
+ result = client.create_chat(
+     prompt="Your task description",
+     task_label="my-task",
+     task_label="my-task",
+     chat_mode="agent",              # See Chat Modes below
+     timeout=1800,                   # 30 min (default). Use 3600 for complex jobs.
+ )
+ )
+ )
+ # Result contains everything — just print the message:
+ print(result["message"])            # Full response — always print in full
+ print(result["status"])             # "completed" | "timeout"
+ print(result["is_operating"])       # False when done
+ Works with **any agent framework** — OpenClaw, Cursor, Claude Code, or any Python environment.
+ ### Notify on Completion (OpenClaw Only — Fire-and-Forget)
+ For OpenClaw agents that want to continue working while CellCog processes in the background. Returns immediately; a background daemon delivers results to your session via `sessions_send` when done. This was CellCog's original delivery mode and enables high-productivity workflows where your agent never blocks.
+     notify_session_key="agent:main:main",   # OpenClaw session key
+     chat_mode="agent",
+ # Returns immediately — daemon notifies your session when complete
+ **Requires OpenClaw Gateway with `sessions_send` enabled** (disabled by default since OpenClaw 2026.4). See the Gateway Configuration section below. The SDK checks this before creating the chat and raises `GatewayConfigError` if blocked.
+ # Wait mode (default)
+ result = client.send_message(
+     chat_id="abc123",
+     message="Focus on hardware advances specifically",
+ # Notify mode (OpenClaw)
+     task_label="continue-research",
+ ### Resuming After Timeout
+ If `create_chat()` or `wait_for_completion()` times out, CellCog is still working. The timeout response includes recent progress so you can decide whether to keep waiting:
+ # Resume waiting on the same chat
+ completion = client.wait_for_completion(chat_id="abc123", timeout=1800)
